-- title:
--   The two branch points of the special fibre are distinct
-- statement:
--   Fix natural numbers $N_0$ and $q$ with $N_0 \neq 0$ and $q$ prime, together with a proof $hqN$ that $q \nmid N_0$, and let $\mathfrak X$ be a Deligne–Rapoport model package `DRModelPackageLevel N₀ q hqN`: a bundle of data for the Igusa-type scheme $X(N_0,q)$ over $\operatorname{Spec} R_q$ consisting of properness, flatness, integrality and local finite presentation of the structure morphism `toBase N₀ q`, integral closedness of the sections over affine opens, a smooth proper curve model over $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ with function field the base-changed modular function field of level $N_0q$ together with its identification with the generic geometric fibre, a Galois-equivariance condition and a pinning of the affine chart coordinates, smoothness of relative dimension $1$ and geometric integrality of the rational fibre, and sections $\varepsilon_\infty,\varepsilon_0$ of the structure morphism, among further fields. Let $O$ be a local commutative ring equipped with a ring homomorphism $\rho_O \colon R_q \to O$ whose maximal ideal is generated by the image of $q$, let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$ with decidable equality, and let $O \to \kappa$ be a ring homomorphism. The conclusion is that the two points $\mathfrak X.\xi_{\infty}$ and $\mathfrak X.\xi_{0}$ attached to $\rho_O$ and to $O \to \kappa$, the generic points of the two branches of the special fibre viewed in the base change of the model to $O$, are distinct.
--
--   This is the level-$(N_0,q)$ form of the statement that the geometric special fibre of the Deligne–Rapoport model of $X_0(N_0q)$ at $q$ is the union of two distinct components, each a copy of $X_0(N_0)_\kappa$, crossing at the supersingular points; the two branch generic points remain distinct after pushing forward to the model over $O$. It is used in the analysis of the crossing frame, in particular for the computation of the branch ideals at the crossing point and for the construction of resolved model packages.
